  {
   "id": 510764,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the error measures are scale-dependent? What would this imply about a similar time series and model in which all values are multiplied by 10?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "See Solution Manual"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<table>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><span data-math=\"x_n\"></span></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><span data-math=\"{\\widehat x}_n\"></span></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>Abs. Residuals</b> <span data-math=\"{\\vert\\varepsilon}_n\\vert={\\vert x}_n-{\\widehat x}_n\\vert\"></span></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><span data-math=\"{{(\\varepsilon}_n)}^2\"></span></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><span data-math=\"\\vert\\frac{\\varepsilon_i}{x_i}\\vert\"></span></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><span data-math=\"\\frac{2\\vert\\varepsilon_i\\vert}{{\\vert x_i\\vert}+\\vert{\\widehat x}_i\\vert}\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">122</td>\n<td align=\"center\">117</td>\n<td align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">25</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.041</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.042</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">108</td>\n<td align=\"center\">135</td>\n<td align=\"center\">27</td>\n<td align=\"center\">729</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.25</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.222</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">172</td>\n<td align=\"center\">152</td>\n<td align=\"center\">20</td>\n<td align=\"center\">400</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.116</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.123</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">190</td>\n<td align=\"center\">169</td>\n<td align=\"center\">21</td>\n<td align=\"center\">441</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.111</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.117</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">167</td>\n<td align=\"center\">186</td>\n<td align=\"center\">19</td>\n<td align=\"center\">361</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.114</td>\n<td align=\"center\">0.108</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>Average:</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>18.4</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>391.2</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>0.126</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><b>0.122</b></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\nComputations to Determine MAE, RMSE, MAPE, and sMAPE<br>\n<span data-math=\"\\text{MAE}=\\text{Mean of&nbsp;}{\\vert\\varepsilon}_n\\vert\\text{&nbsp;terms}\"></span>: 18.4. <span data-math=\"\\text{RMSE}=\\text{Square root of the mean of the&nbsp;}{{(\\varepsilon}_n)}^2\\text{&nbsp;terms}\"></span>: 19.78. <span data-math=\"\\text{MAPE}=\\text{Mean of&nbsp;}\\vert\\frac{\\varepsilon_i}{x_i}\\vert\\text{&nbsp;terms}\"></span>: 0.126 (12.6%). <span data-math=\"\\text{sMAPE}=\\text{Mean of&nbsp;}\\frac{2\\vert\\varepsilon_i\\vert}{\\vert x_i\\vert+\\vert{\\widehat x}_i\\vert}\"></span> terms: 0.122 (12.2%).<br>\nMAE and RMSE are scale-dependent. This means that the error generally scales in proportion to the data. Thus, if both the time series and model values were multiplied by 10, then the MAE and RMSE would become 184 and 197.8, respectively."
    }
